---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { commonMembers, problems, problemTypeUri } from "../../data/problems";
---

<BaseLayout title="Problem types" description="The RFC 9457 problem types of the qyl API contract: one page per type URI, with its code, HTTP status and members.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="reading-shell">
      <span class="eyebrow">Problem types · RFC 9457</span>
      <h1 class="page-title">The errors of the qyl API, by stable type.</h1>
      <p class="lede">When the Collector or the qyl runner refuses a request, it sends an RFC 9457 problem body as <code>application/problem+json</code>. Apart from the three answers named at the end of this page, its <code>type</code> member is one of the URIs below, and its <code>code</code> member names the same type as a short string. Branch on either; never on <code>title</code> or <code>detail</code>, which are written for people.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="editorial-section editorial-section--raised">
    <article class="reading-shell docs-content">
      <h2>The types</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Code</th><th scope="col">Status</th><th scope="col">When</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {problems.map((problem) => (
            <tr>
              <td><a href={`/problems/${problem.name}/`}>{problem.title}</a></td>
              <td><code>{problem.code}</code></td>
              <td>{problem.status}</td>
              <td>{problem.summary}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Each type's URI is <code>{problemTypeUri("<name>")}</code>, and that URI is the page the row links to.</p>

      <h2>Members every problem carries</h2>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Member</th><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {commonMembers.map((member) => (
            <tr><td><code>{member.name}</code></td><td>{member.type}</td><td>{member.description}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>A type may add members of its own; each type's page lists them. Headers are a property of the response, not of the body: the 500 of an unexpected Collector failure carries <code>X-Trace-Id</code>.</p>

      <h2>Where problems come from</h2>
      <p>The list is fixed by the qyl API contract, which the Collector's API at <code>api.qyl.at</code>, the local runner API of the qyl CLI and the <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP server</a> share. Three answers fall outside it: the Collector reports an analysis that runs out of time with the service-unavailable type and HTTP 504, a failure lookup that exceeds its scan budget with a plain HTTP 503 problem that carries no qyl type, and the runner answers an unknown route or method with a bare 404 or 405. A tool call the Collector refused returns its problem type as <code>problem_type</code> in the call's <code>structuredContent.error</code>.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
